Z||000005||kslish||08-01-2001||12:15 AM||||Sounds like your starter solenoid is suffering from heat soak (the solenoid is the smaller cylinder attached to the starter).  My '86 coupe does this too on occasion (started doing it this year going to the Carlisle show).  What happens is the solenoid gets cooked by exhaust and engine heat and after years of this abuse they begin to refuse to enguage when hot.  If the starter solenoid doesn't enguage, no power goes to the starter to start the car.  Once the solenoid cools off, the car starts normally.<P>I ended up putting a solenoid heat shield on mine and it helped.  Now it only does it on really hot days after cruising for hours on the highway at 75+ miles an hour....around town it's fine.  However, the correct fix is to replace the solenoid with a new one....which is what I plan on doing eventually this winter.  I've just been to lazy to pull the starter to replace mine.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
